
# Task 8: Iterative Methods for Linear Systems

## 1. Objective
The aim is to solve systems of linear equations using the Jacobi iterative method.

## 2. Inputs
The program uses the equations represented by:

A = [[10, 1, 1], [2, 10, 1], [2, 2, 10]]

B = [12, 13, 14]

## 3. Mathematical Operations
The program uses the Jacobi iterative method to estimate the unknown values by repeatedly updating the solutions until the required accuracy is reached.

## 4. Original Output
The solution is:
x = 1, y = 1, z = 1.

Number of iterations = 13.

## 5. Modified Input
The first constant was changed from 12 to 13.

## 6. Observation
The modified input produces different solution values. Both calculations take 13 iterations to reach the required accuracy.

## 7. Interpretation
The Jacobi method solves equations through repeated calculations until the solution becomes sufficiently accurate.

## 8. Related Real-World Problem
The method can be used in engineering simulations and electrical network calculations.

## 9. Assumptions
The coefficient matrix has non-zero diagonal values and is diagonally dominant, allowing the method to converge for this system.

## 10. New Input Data
The modified constants are:

B = [13, 13, 14]

## 11. Output
The modified solution is:
x = 1.104, y = 0.981, z = 0.983.

Number of iterations = 13.

## 12. Interpretation of the Modified Problem
Changing the first constant changes the estimated values of the unknown variables. This demonstrates how iterative methods can solve systems of linear equations.
